  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAs anticipated and forecasted by the models, a weakening and dying \nMCS is currently entering the forecast area from KY. Lightning \nactivity has greatly fallen off before it reached our CWA border. We \ndo not anticipate severe storms, but a minor gust of wind cannot be \nruled out. Middlesboro, KY just after 12:30 gusted to almost 30 mph. \nDecent CAPE, DCAPE, and EBShear are present for this time of night; \n2500 J/Kg, and almost 40 KT Effective.\n\nLater this morning into the afternoon and possibly early evening \nhours, still holds fairly low confidence on evolution of activity \ntoday in regards to, strength, timing, location, and hazards besides \ndamaging wind gusts. Timing for the frontal passage itself will be \nbetween 2 and 8 pm ET. The latest HRRR depicts another round of \nshowers and storms will barrel down from the Ohio Valley region \naround sunrise and after. A few samplings of forecast soundings over \nsouthwest VA show strong to severe storm capability, with increasing \nvertical shear. There may be parts of the forecast area, in \nparticular, southwest VA, that may see repeated rounds of \nprecipitation from this morning into the afternoon, which raises the \nconcern for flooding from training storms. QPF totals vary from the \nHRRR to the RRFS, though the RRFS tries to bring chances a bit \nfurther south, meanwhile, the HRRR keeps activity restricted to the \nnortheastern sector of the CWA. In all, the instability will surely \nbe there (will it be impacted by morning activity?), moisture is \nsurely present, and forcing coming from the front will bring \nscattered showers and thunderstorms for much of the day with some \nstrong to severe capability. The main hazard still remains damaging \nwind gusts, with isolated flash flooding from heavy rainfall and \npossible training. Smaller than 1 inch hail could be possible, as \nwell as well as a tornado, given the increase in shear and SRH later \ntoday.\n\nBefore getting into the rest of the forecast period, which won't be \nmuch to touch on at all, it will be hot and humid again later today. \nSouthwest VA into northeast TN may escape the hottest conditions, as \nwell as the Southern Appalachians, but that goes without saying. \nUpper 90s to mid 100s for heat indices today from the central valley \nsouth to the GA border (southern valley).\n\nTomorrow into the first weekend of August will see temperatures \ndropping to just below normal and for the most part, holding steady \nas lower heights from the western side of the trough sink south. \nHumidity will also trend lower into Thursday before a trend upward \nbegins Friday. Surprisingly enough, once we clear things out of here \nlate tonight, dry weather will most likely persist into early \nSaturday before precipitation chances creep back in. Surface high \npressure at that time, begins to be replaced by another shortwave \nlobe and a system possibly moving in from the NW.",
